'''Trichogramminæ''' (trik″ọ̄-gra-mī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (L. O. Howard, 1885), < ''Trichogramma'' + ''-inæ''.] A subfamily of parasitic hymenopters, of the family ''Chalcididæ'', containing the smallest species of the family, characterized by their three-jointed tarsi (thus forming the section ''Trimera'') and the regular fringe of minute bristles on the wings. {{smaller|They vary in color from bright yellow to reddish brown, and are all parasitic in the eggs of other insects. Also ''Trichogrammatoidæ'' (Förster, 1856). See cut under ''Trichogramma''.}}

'''trichogyne''' (trik′ọ̄-jin), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + γυνή, a female.] In ''bot.'', a long thin hair-like sac springing from the trichophoric part of the procarp of certain cryptogams, and serving as a receptive organ of reproduction. See ''procarp'', ''Florideæ''.

'''trichogynic''' (trik-ọ̄-jin′ik), ''a.'' [< ''trichogyne'' + ''-ic''.] In ''bot.'', of or pertaining to the trichogyne.

'''trichologia''' (trik-ọ̄-lō′ji-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. as if *τριχολογία, < τριχολογεῖν, pluck hairs (as a symptom), < θρίξ (τριχ-), hair, + λέγειν, gather, pick.] Carphologia.

